EN[43] = "The frost grew stronger, and up there it became so cold that the devil hopped from one little hoof to the other and blew into his fist, trying to warm his freezing hands a little. No wonder, though, that he should freeze, who had been knocking about from morning to morning in hell, where, as is well known, it is not so cold as with us in winter, and where, putting on a cook's cap and standing before the hearth like a real chef, he roasted sinners with the pleasure with which a housewife usually fries sausage at Christmas. The witch herself felt it was cold, although she was warmly dressed; and so, raising her arms, thrusting out one leg, and putting herself in the position of a man flying on skates, without moving a single joint she slid down through the air as down a slippery icy slope, straight into the chimney. The devil followed her in the same manner. But since this creature is nimbler than any dandy in stockings, it is no wonder that at the very entrance to the chimney he ran into his sweetheart's neck, and both found themselves in the roomy stove among the pots. The traveller quietly pushed back the stove door to look and see whether her son Vakula had invited guests into the cottage, but seeing that there was no one, except the sacks lying in the middle of the room, she climbed out of the stove, threw off her warm sheepskin, tidied herself, and no one could have known that a minute before she had been riding a broom. The mother of the blacksmith Vakula was no more than forty years old. She was neither pretty nor plain. It is hard to be pretty at such an age. Yet she knew how to charm the most respectable Cossacks (who, it does no harm to remark, cared little for beauty), so that the headman used to visit her, and the deacon Osip Nikiforovich (of course, if the deacon's wife was not at home), and the Cossack Korniy Chub, and the Cossack Kasyan Svyerbyhuz. And, to her credit, she knew how to deal with them skilfully. It never entered the head of any of them that he had a rival. Whether a pious peasant, or a gentleman, as the Cossacks call themselves, dressed in a cloak with a hood, was going on Sunday to church, or, if the weather was bad, to the tavern, how could he not drop in on Solokha, eat the rich dumplings with sour cream, and chat in a warm cottage with the talkative, obliging hostess? And the gentleman would make a big detour on purpose before reaching the tavern, and call it dropping in on the way. And when Solokha went to church on a holiday, in a bright plakhta with a Chinese-cotton zapaska and over it a blue skirt with gold whiskers sewn on the back, and stood right by the right-hand choir, the deacon would surely begin to cough and involuntarily screw up his eyes in that direction; the headman would stroke his moustache, wind his forelock round his ear and say to the neighbour standing near him: “Ah, a fine woman! A devil of a woman!” Solokha bowed to each of them, and each thought she bowed to him alone. But anyone fond of meddling in other people's business would have noticed at once that Solokha was friendliest of all to the Cossack Chub. Chub was a widower; eight stacks of grain always stood before his cottage. Two pairs of sturdy oxen always thrust their heads out of the wattled shed into the street and lowed when they saw the cow, their friend, passing, or Uncle, the fat bull. A bearded goat climbed right up on the roof and bleated from there in a harsh voice, like a mayor, teasing the turkeys that strutted about the yard, and turning his back when he saw his enemies, the boys who made fun of his beard. In Chub's chests there was plenty of linen, coats, and old-fashioned kuntushes with gold braid: his late wife had been a fashionable woman. In the vegetable garden, besides poppies, cabbages and sunflowers, two fields of tobacco were sown every year. All this Solokha thought it no bad thing to add to her own household, considering beforehand what order it would take when it passed into her hands, and she doubled her kindness to old Chub. And so that her son Vakula might not somehow get at his daughter and manage to take everything himself, in which case he would certainly not let her meddle in anything, she resorted to the usual means of all forty-year-old gossips: to set Chub and the blacksmith at odds as often as possible. Perhaps it was these very tricks and her shrewdness that were the cause of old women beginning here and there to say, especially when they had drunk a drop too much at some merry gathering, that Solokha really was a witch; that the lad Kizyakolupenko had seen a tail on her behind no bigger than a woman's spindle; that only the Thursday before last she had run across the road as a black cat; that once a pig had run to the priest's wife, crowed like a cock, put Father Kondrat's hat on its head and run back. It happened that while the old women were discussing this, the cowherd Tymish Korostyavy came along. He did not fail to tell how in summer, just before Saint Peter's day, when he had lain down to sleep in the cattle shed with straw under his head, he had seen with his own eyes a witch with her hair loose, in her shift alone, begin to milk the cows, and he could not stir, so bewitched was he; when she had milked the cows, she came to him and smeared his lips with something so foul that he spat for a whole day afterwards. But all this is somewhat doubtful, because only the Sorochintsy assessor can see a witch. And so all the distinguished Cossacks waved their hands when they heard such talk. “They are lying, the bitches!” was their usual answer."
EN[44] = "When she had climbed out of the stove and tidied herself, Solokha, like a good housewife, began to clear up and put everything in its place; but she did not touch the sacks: Vakula had brought them, so let him carry them out himself! The devil meanwhile, as he flew into the chimney, happened to turn round and saw Chub arm in arm with his friend, already far from the cottage. In a moment he flew out of the stove, ran across their path, and began to tear up the heaps of frozen snow on all sides. A blizzard rose. The air turned white. The snow whirled back and forth like a net and threatened to plaster the eyes, mouth and ears of the walkers. And the devil flew back into the chimney, firmly convinced that Chub would turn back with his friend, find the blacksmith, and give him such a welcome that for a long time he would be unable to take a brush in his hand and paint insulting caricatures."
EN[46] = "In fact, no sooner had the blizzard risen and the wind begun to cut straight into his eyes than Chub declared his regret and, pulling his cap lower on his head, treated himself, the devil and his friend to curses. However, this annoyance was feigned. Chub was very glad of the blizzard. To the deacon's there was still eight times as much distance as they had covered. The travellers turned back. The wind blew on the back of their heads; but through the sweeping snow nothing could be seen."
EN[47] = "<<CHUB>>“Stop, friend! I think we are not going the right way,”<<N>> said Chub, after going a little distance. <<CHUB>>“I don't see a single cottage. Ugh, what a blizzard! Turn a little to the side, friend, perhaps you will find the road; and in the meantime I will look here. The unclean spirit must have driven us to trudge about in such a storm! Don't forget to shout when you find the road. Ugh, what a heap of snow Satan has thrown in my eyes!”"
EN[48] = "The road, however, was nowhere to be seen. The friend, moving off to the side, wandered back and forth in his long boots and at last came straight upon the tavern. This find so delighted him that he forgot everything and, shaking off the snow, went into the porch, not troubling himself in the least about his friend left in the street. To Chub meanwhile it seemed that he had found the road; stopping, he began to shout at the top of his voice, but seeing that his friend did not appear, he decided to go on alone. After going a little way he saw his cottage. Drifts of snow lay around it and on the roof. Clapping his hands, numb with cold, he began to knock at the door and shout, in a commanding voice, to his daughter to open it."
EN[49] = "<<VAKULA>>“What do you want here?”<<N>> shouted the blacksmith, coming out, sternly."
EN[50] = "Chub, recognising the blacksmith's voice, stepped back a little. <<CHUB>>“Ah, no, this is not my cottage,”<<N>> he said to himself, <<CHUB>>“the blacksmith would not stray into my cottage. And then, if one looks carefully, it is not the blacksmith's either. Whose cottage could it be? There now! I didn't recognise it! This is lame Levchenko's, who has lately married a young wife. His is the only cottage like mine. That is why it seemed a little strange to me at first that I had come home so quickly. But Levchenko is sitting at the deacon's now, I know that; so why is the blacksmith here? Aha, ha, ha! He is visiting his young wife. So that's how it is! Good! Now I understand it all.”"
EN[51] = "<<VAKULA>>“Who are you, and why are you hanging about at the door?”<<N>> said the blacksmith, more sternly than before, coming closer."
EN[52] = "<<CHUB>>“No, I won't tell him who I am,”<<N>> thought Chub. <<CHUB>>“Heaven knows, the accursed brute may even thrash me!”<<N>> And, changing his voice, he answered: <<CHUB>>“It is I, a good man, who have come to sing carols a little under your windows, for your amusement.”"
EN[53] = "<<VAKULA>>“Go to the devil with your carols!”<<N>> shouted Vakula angrily. <<VAKULA>>“Why are you standing there? Do you hear, get out of here this minute!”"
EN[54] = "Chub himself already had this sensible intention; but it seemed annoying to him to be forced to obey the blacksmith's orders. It seemed that some evil spirit jogged his arm and forced him to say something contrary. <<CHUB>>“Why are you really shouting so?”<<N>> he said in the same voice. <<CHUB>>“I want to sing carols, and that's all.”"
EN[55] = "<<VAKULA>>“Aha! You won't stop at words!”<<N>> With these words Chub felt a very painful blow on the shoulder."
EN[56] = "<<CHUB>>“So you are beginning to fight, I see!”<<N>> he said, backing away a little."
EN[57] = "<<VAKULA>>“Go, go!”<<N>> shouted the blacksmith, rewarding Chub with another shove."
EN[58] = "<<CHUB>>“What are you doing!”<<N>> said Chub, in a voice that expressed pain, and annoyance, and timidity. <<CHUB>>“You are not fighting in jest, I see, and you hit hard, too!”"
EN[59] = "<<VAKULA>>“Go, go!”<<N>> shouted the blacksmith and slammed the door."
EN[60] = "<<CHUB>>“Look how brave he has become!”<<N>> said Chub, left alone in the street. <<CHUB>>“Just try to come near! Look at him! What a great fine fellow! You think I won't find justice against you. No, my dear, I will go, and go straight to the commissary. You will learn from me. I won't look at your being a blacksmith and a painter. But let me look at my back and shoulders: I think there are blue marks. He must have beaten me hard, the enemy's son! A pity it is cold and I don't want to take off my sheepskin! Just wait, you devilish blacksmith, may the devil beat you and your forge, you will dance for this! Look at the accursed rascal! But then, he is not at home now. Solokha, I think, is sitting alone. Hm... it is not far from here; I might go! It is the sort of time that no one will catch us. Perhaps it will even be possible to... look how hard he beat me, the accursed blacksmith!”"
EN[61] = "Here Chub, scratching his back, set off in another direction. The pleasure awaiting him ahead in his meeting with Solokha eased the pain a little and made him insensible even to the frost, which crackled in all the streets, unmuffled by the whistling of the blizzard. From time to time on his face, whose beard and moustache the storm had lathered with snow more quickly than any barber who tyrannically seizes his victim by the nose, a half-sweet expression appeared. But if the snow had not been crossing and recrossing everything before his eyes, one could have seen for a long time how Chub stopped, scratched his back, said, “He beat me hard, the accursed blacksmith!” and set off on his way again."
EN[63] = "At the time when the nimble dandy with the tail and goat's beard was flying out of the chimney and then back into the chimney, the pouch that hung at his side on a strap, into which he had hidden the stolen moon, somehow happened to catch in the stove and came open, and the moon, taking advantage of the chance, flew out through the chimney of Solokha's cottage and rose smoothly into the sky. Everything was lit up. The blizzard was gone as if it had never been. The snow blazed into a wide silver field and was all sprinkled with crystal stars. The frost seemed to grow warmer. Crowds of lads and girls appeared with sacks. Songs rang out, and there was scarcely a cottage under whose windows carollers were not crowding. How wonderfully the moon shines! It is hard to tell how good it is to knock about, on such a night, among a crowd of laughing, singing girls and among lads ready for any joke and invention that a merrily laughing night can inspire. Under a thick sheepskin it is warm; the frost makes the cheeks burn even brighter; and for mischief the sly one himself pushes you from behind. Crowds of girls with sacks burst into Chub's cottage and surrounded Oksana. Shouts, laughter, stories deafened the blacksmith. They all vied in hurrying to tell the beauty something new, unloaded their sacks and boasted of the loaves, sausages and dumplings which they had already managed to collect in plenty for their carols. Oksana seemed to be in perfect pleasure and joy, chattered now with one, now with another, and laughed without stopping. With a sort of vexation and envy the blacksmith looked at such merriment and, this time, cursed the carols, although he himself was usually mad about them."
EN[64] = "<<OKSANA>>“Ah, Odarka!”<<N>> said the merry beauty, turning to one of the girls. <<OKSANA>>“You have new shoes! Oh, how pretty! And with gold! It is good for you, Odarka, you have a man who buys you everything; and I have no one to get such lovely shoes for me.”"
EN[65] = "<<VAKULA>>“Don't grieve, my dearest Oksana!”<<N>> the blacksmith broke in. <<VAKULA>>“I will get you such shoes as few young ladies wear.”"
EN[66] = "<<OKSANA>>“You?”<<N>> said Oksana, glancing quickly and haughtily at him. <<OKSANA>>“I shall see where you will get shoes that I could put on my foot. Unless you bring me the very ones the Empress wears.”"
EN[67] = "<<GIRLS>>“Look what she has asked for!”<<N>> cried the crowd of girls, laughing."
EN[68] = "<<OKSANA>>“Yes!”<<N>> the beauty went on proudly. <<OKSANA>>“Be witnesses, all of you: if the blacksmith Vakula brings me the very shoes the Empress wears, then here is my word that I will marry him that very hour.”"
EN[69] = "The girls led the capricious beauty away with them."
EN[70] = "<<VAKULA>>“Laugh, laugh!”<<N>> said the blacksmith, going out after them. <<VAKULA>>“I laugh at myself! I think and cannot think where my wits have gone. She does not love me, well, God be with her! As if there were only one Oksana in all the world. Thank God, there are plenty of good girls in the village without her. And what is Oksana? She will never make a good housewife; she is only a master at dressing up. No, enough, it is time to stop playing the fool.”<<N>> But at the very moment when the blacksmith was preparing to be resolute, some evil spirit carried before him the laughing image of Oksana, saying mockingly: <<OKSANA>>“Get me the Empress's shoes, blacksmith, and I will marry you!”<<N>> Everything in him was stirred, and he thought of Oksana alone."
EN[71] = "Crowds of carollers, the lads by themselves, the girls by themselves, hurried from one street to another. But the blacksmith walked and saw nothing and took no part in the merriment that he had once loved more than anyone."
